Assigns a colour to every level of a taxonomic rank -- or, when \code{n} is
given, to the \code{n} heaviest of them plus a reserved colour for the rest --
and returns the result as a named vector that can be reused across every
figure of a document.

Because \pkg{ggplot2} matches manual scales \emph{by name}, a plot showing only
some of the levels simply consumes the matching entries: the colours never
shift from one figure to the next. Anchor the palette once on the complete
object, then reuse it on any subset.
}
\section{Filtering is opt-in}{
By default (\code{n = NULL}) every level of the rank gets its own colour and
\strong{\code{filter_by} is never consulted}. Filtering only starts when you set \code{n},
and \code{filter_by} then decides which levels survive. The two arguments are
therefore not independent: setting \code{filter_by} without setting \code{n} has no
effect at all.

\code{order_by} is different -- it always applies, because the levels have to be
listed in some order whether or not any were dropped.

A rank with many levels will hit the palette guards: a warning past 25
colours, an error past 40. That is the signal to set \code{n}.
}

\section{Where the palette lives}{
With \code{add_to_phyloseq = TRUE} the colours are written into a
\verb{<rank><suffix>} column of the \code{tax_table} and the \strong{phyloseq object} is
returned instead of the palette. Every taxon gets a colour, including those
outside the top \code{n}, which take the reserved \code{other} entry. The column then
travels with the rows through \code{subset_taxa()}, \code{prune_taxa()} and
\code{saveRDS()}, so the anchoring survives without any variable being carried
around the document.

On a later call, an existing column is \strong{read back} rather than recomputed,
which is what makes the colours stable. Writing over it requires either
\code{force = TRUE}, or a different \code{suffix} so both palettes coexist.
}

\section{Nested palettes}{
With \code{nested}, hue encodes the parent rank and lightness the rank of the
leaf within its parent, so a bar chart shows the phylum-level structure at a
glance. Keys are the leaf names alone; a leaf name occurring under several
parents is disambiguated according to \code{on_duplicate}.

A nested palette is \strong{more} dependent on anchoring than a flat one, not
less. Recomputing it on a subset shifts the leaf shades, because each
gradient is spread over the siblings actually present, and shifts the parent
hues too whenever a whole parent drops out and the hue circle is divided
differently. Anchor once on the complete object -- keep the returned
palette, or store it with \code{add_to_phyloseq = TRUE} -- and reuse it. That is
what the anchoring is for; it is not a fallback.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
data(data_fungi_mini, package = "MiscMetabar")

# Every Order gets a colour, listed alphabetically
pal <- palette_tax_pq(data_fungi_mini, "Order")
pal

# Keep only the 4 most abundant; the rest turn grey
palette_tax_pq(data_fungi_mini, "Order", n = 4)

# Keep the 4 carrying the most taxa instead
palette_tax_pq(data_fungi_mini, "Order", n = 4, filter_by = "n_taxa")

# The same palette on a subset: the surviving levels keep their colours
sub <- phyloseq::prune_taxa(
  phyloseq::taxa_sums(data_fungi_mini) > 100,
  data_fungi_mini
)
palette_tax_pq(sub, "Order")
}

